<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Mycenaean Kingdom of Thebes</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.) · capital Thebes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mycenaean palace kingdom on the Kadmeia hill in Boeotia, whose Linear B archive and Near Eastern treasures mark it as one of the major Bronze Age Greek powers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> occupies the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Kadmeia</a>, a low plateau between fertile plains in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. A palace centre emerged there by c. 1600 BCE, and by the 14th century BCE its rulers controlled much of Boeotia and perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. Greek myth gave the citadel its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a>, its legendary Phoenician founder.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Excavations under the modern town have uncovered parts of the palace, workshops for jewellery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and a hoard of Near Eastern cylinder seals of lapis lazuli. Several hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets record offerings to gods, wool, food rations and places as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. Some scholars argue that <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> was the centre of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ahhiyawa">Ahhiyawa</a> named in Hittite texts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The palace was destroyed by fire in the 13th century BCE, the setting later remembered in the myths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oedipus">Oedipus</a> and the Seven against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a>. The city remained inhabited and became the leading Boeotian city-state, described separately as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1600 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:27.86%;width:7.96%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thebes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mycenaean Thebes, Kadmeia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1400&amp;focus=mycenaean-kingdom-of-thebes" aria-label="Mycenaean Kingdom of Thebes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5260 801 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mycenaean Kingdom of Thebes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5580 1057h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5580 1057h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Thebes. <a href="/atlas?year=-1400&amp;focus=mycenaean-kingdom-of-thebes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

